"""Build independent report narratives from verified warehouse outputs."""
from pathlib import Path
import csv,json,re
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
for student in 'AB':
 p=ROOT/'submission'/f'Student_{student}'
 rows={q:list(csv.DictReader((p/'evidence/sql'/f'{q}-0.csv').open(encoding='utf-8-sig'))) for q in ['Q4_1','Q4_2','Q4_3']}
 assert len(rows['Q4_1'])==8 and len(rows['Q4_2'])==40
 if student=='A':
  summary='OzMart’s warehouse combines 137,901 order items in a star schema for customer, product, supplier, calendar and regional analysis. Delivered orders provide a completed-sales measure. Profit cannot be estimated without product costs and freight.\n\nThe leading age group by revenue varies across states, from age 19 in ACT to age 85 in Tasmania. Toys & Games leads delivered unit sales in all eight states, including 24,667 units in NSW. The supplier query returns five NSW-based suppliers for each year. Erin Marquez leads 2022 and 2023 with 833 and 598 units. These findings can inform regional stock and supplier reviews, although they describe the supplied period rather than future demand.\n\nThe loan analysis compares logistic regression and a decision tree using 9,998 labelled records, an 80/20 stratified split and five-fold cross-validation within training data. Logistic regression has test AUC 0.625, compared with 0.500 for the tree. Both miss every held-out default. Accuracy around 89% therefore provides little assurance about credit risk. Neither model should determine lending eligibility. Further analysis requires predictors recorded before default, training-based threshold selection and independent validation, supported by privacy safeguards and human review.'
  q1='Revenue is SUM(LineAmount) for delivered items, grouped by customer destination state and exact source age. DENSE_RANK retains all age groups sharing the highest revenue (Microsoft, 2026). The result contains one winner per state: ACT 19, NSW 57, NT 71, QLD 40, SA 81, TAS 85, VIC 35 and WA 31. NSW age 57 contributes 44,167.3311 in the source currency. These are aggregate revenue profiles, not per-customer spending estimates; cohort size and purchase frequency may explain differences. No currency conversion or causal targeting effect is assumed.'
  q2='Product type means the product category, and best-selling means delivered units rather than revenue or distinct order lines. Ranking is partitioned by customer destination state; DENSE_RANK retains tied category positions. The actual output has five categories in each of eight states, with no included ties. Toys & Games ranks first everywhere: NSW contributes 24,667 units, VIC 17,914 and QLD 17,836. Revenue is shown as supporting context. The result supports category-level stock review, but does not reveal margins, inventory availability or future demand.'
  q3='The query defines NSW suppliers by their own location and sums delivered quantities by purchase year. ROW_NUMBER orders units descending, then SellerID ascending to resolve ties consistently. It returns exactly five suppliers in each of 2021, 2022 and 2023: 15 rows overall. Michelle Wells leads 2021 with 23 units; Erin Marquez leads 2022 and 2023 with 833 and 598. The conflicting location recorded as UNK is excluded because its state cannot be established. Supplier location answers the question about NSW-based providers; customer destination would describe a different market. Annual differences may partly reflect unequal observation periods.'
 else:
  summary='The retail analysis uses a separate warehouse containing 137,901 original order items and five dimensions. Eleven native SSIS data flows load reference data, dimensions and facts. The customer integration uses sorted Merge Join inputs, while warehouse measures retain both raw source values and explicitly rounded amounts. Delivered orders form the sales analysis population. Profit cannot be calculated reliably without costs and freight.\n\nThe strongest exact-age revenue profile varies by destination state, from age 19 in ACT to age 85 in Tasmania. Toys & Games is the unit-sales leader in every state; NSW records 24,667 delivered units. Among NSW-origin suppliers, Michelle Wells leads 2021 with 23 units and Erin Marquez leads 2022 and 2023 with 833 and 598. Deterministic row numbering returns exactly five suppliers per year and resolves quantity ties by seller identity.\n\nThe loan experiment reserves 2,999 of 9,998 eligible customers through a stratified 70/30 split. Training-only cross-validation compares logistic regression with a 50-tree random forest. Both fixed test classifiers miss every default. Logistic regression’s test AUC is 0.595 versus the forest’s 0.500; approximately 89% accuracy reflects class imbalance. The evidence supports further research, not operational eligibility decisions. New temporal data, loss-sensitive thresholds defined before validation, subgroup checks and accountable human review are necessary before deployment could be considered.'
  q1='The query aggregates delivered LineAmount by destination state and exact customer age. ROW_NUMBER partitions by state, orders revenue descending and uses age ascending as the deterministic tie-breaker (Microsoft, n.d.-a). Each state therefore returns one profile. The observed winning ages are 19, 57, 71, 40, 81, 85, 35 and 31 for ACT, NSW, NT, QLD, SA, TAS, VIC and WA respectively. NSW age 57 has revenue 44,167.3311 in the supplied currency. Aggregate totals cannot distinguish population size from individual spending, so they do not establish that an age group is intrinsically more valuable.'
  q2='The category dimension defines product types. SUM(ItemQuantity) measures best-selling categories among delivered items, grouped by customer destination state. ROW_NUMBER uses category ID as a stable secondary ordering and returns exactly five categories where available. There are 40 result rows. Toys & Games leads all eight states; its units range from 255 in ACT to 24,667 in NSW. VIC and QLD record 17,914 and 17,836 units. These regional volumes can guide a stock-planning investigation, although costs, stockouts and the length of observation would be needed for an investment decision.'
  q3='The main analysis identifies sellers located in NSW and totals their delivered item quantities within purchase year. ROW_NUMBER sorts quantity descending and SellerID ascending. It returns 15 rows: five suppliers for each of 2021, 2022 and 2023. Michelle Wells sells 23 units as the 2021 leader. Erin Marquez leads the following two years at 833 and 598 units. In 2021, Amy Trevino and Jason Long both sell 21 units; seller identity decides their third and fourth positions. Supplier origin identifies NSW-based providers; customer destination would answer a different question. Differences between calendar years may reflect observation coverage as well as sales activity.'
 out={'sections':{'executive_summary':summary,'query_4_1':q1,'query_4_2':q2,'query_4_3':q3},'word_counts':{'executive_summary':len(summary.split())},'source_rows':{q:len(v) for q,v in rows.items()}}
 assert out['word_counts']['executive_summary']<=250
 (ROOT/'docs/report_source'/f'Student_{student}'/'query_sections.json').write_text(json.dumps(out,indent=2)+'\n')
 print(student,out['word_counts'])
